3. `_assert_read_only_sql` — READ-ONLY ENFORCEMENT (THE CRITICAL PIECE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verified fact (T6/T7 learnings, re-confirmed): duckdb 1.5.5 CANNOT flip a
running in-memory connection to read-only. `SET access_mode='read_only'` (and
SET GLOBAL) raise InvalidInputException ("it must be set when opening or
attaching"); `duckdb.connect(":memory:", read_only=True)` raises
CatalogException ("Cannot launch in-memory database in read-only mode!");
`ATTACH ':memory:' (READ_ONLY)` fails too. => enforcement is STATEMENT-LEVEL.

Implementation (comment stripping -> semicolon rule -> keyword prefix check):

    def _assert_read_only_sql(sql):
        stripped = re.sub(r"/\*.*?\*/", "", sql, flags=re.DOTALL)   # block cmts
        stripped = re.sub(r"--[^\n]*", "", stripped)                # line cmts
        stripped = stripped.strip()
        if stripped.endswith(";"):
            stripped = stripped[:-1].strip()                        # <=1 trailing ;
        if ";" in stripped:
            raise ValueError("only SELECT queries are allowed")     # multi-stmt
        first_word = re.split(r"\s+", stripped, maxsplit=1)[0].lower()
        # first word must be a PREFIX of SELECT or WITH (case-insensitive):
        if not ("select".startswith(first_word) or "with".startswith(first_word)):
            raise ValueError("only SELECT queries are allowed")
        return stripped

Design decisions:
- COMMENT STRIPPING happens BEFORE the keyword/semicolon checks, so
  `-- x\nDROP TABLE` and `/* x */ DROP TABLE` are both rejected. Both regexes
  are non-greedy / to-end-of-line; a `;` hidden inside a string literal is
  over-strictly rejected (safe failure, never a false accept).
- KEYWORD CHECK = "first word is a prefix of select/with". Rationale: the
  spec's syntax-error test requires `SELEC 1` to reach the DuckDB parser
  (-> "SQL error: ..."). A strict prefix (selec, w, ...) can NEVER begin a
  valid DuckDB statement, so only exact `select`/`with` first words execute;
  truncated-keyword typos fall through to DuckDB's ParserException. All real
  destructive keywords (DROP/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/ALTER/CREATE/...) are
  rejected because their first word is not a select/with prefix.
- SEMICOLON RULE: at most ONE trailing `;` (stripped before validation);
  any remaining `;` (e.g. `SELECT 1; DROP TABLE nodes`) is rejected.
- KNOWN RESIDUAL (documented, acceptable): `SELECT * INTO t FROM nodes`
  creates a table via the SELECT keyword. The plan's design is statement-level
  best-effort; the agent is trusted (per task spec: "Use conn.execute(sql)
  directly (the agent is trusted)"). Not a security boundary.

